Fillets: at an end where the fillet is cut by the faces round the vertex
(PerformIntersectionAtEnd), a crossing with the common boundary of two of
those faces past the end of the edge the walk round the vertex reached
it at, where that boundary goes on in another edge of both faces, is
taken on that edge (mitcad#1615).

A flange 2 thick whose end cap stops 2 short of the flange's tip, the
inner corner rounded with 5 and the outer edge of the flange's foot and
the end face rounded with 7 along its whole length: past the cap, over
the last 2, the rounding's contact on the end face (7 from the edge)
lies beyond that face's narrow part (the flange's end, 2 wide), and the
end is cut by the faces round the notch: the cap's side, the flange's
inside and the flange's tip. The flange's inside meets the cap's side
along a straight boundary split at the cap's inner corner (2 up), the
edge below it from the notch's floor (the end face) and the one above
from the inner rounding; the rounding crosses that boundary at
7 - sqrt(4 r - 4) = 2.101 up for r = 7, past the first edge's end
(above r = 4 + 2 sqrt(2) = 6.83). The crossing beyond an edge's end
was taken for one on the prolongation of the faces' common boundary
(where the two faces part there): a new edge from the vertex to the
crossing was made along the first edge's curve while the second edge,
over it, stayed whole in both faces, and the flange's inside kept its
part under the rounding with the notch floor's edge, which lost its
other face: the result had a free edge, an open shell.

- ChFi3d_CrossingOnCommonBoundary: from the vertex past which the
  crossing lies, the edges of both faces that go on from it in turn, up
  to the first the blend crosses within its range (at most eight): that
  edge, the crossing's parameter on it and point on the blend, and the
  edges passed on the way with their vertices towards the crossing.
- PerformIntersectionAtEnd: where such an edge is found (not at the
  corner's vertex nor at the previous edge's vertex, where the earlier
  handling stays), it is the edge of that step and the crossing lies on
  it, cut there as any crossing on its edge, its end towards the corner
  the one the chain reached it at; each edge passed is cut at its end
  towards the crossing, the whole of it removed with the corner (as the
  edges between a prolonged edge's end and the vertex are).

